  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">High Level Forum on Public Procurement Reforms in Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The November 2009 Forum was organized in partnership with the Common Market for Eastern and Southern Africa (COMESA), the West African Economic and Monetary Union (WAEMU), the African Capacity Building Foundation (ACBF), the Organization for Economic Co-Operation and Development (OECD) and the World Bank (WB). The four themes discussed during the Forum included the role of Public Procurement reforms in Africa and its role as a tool for Economic Development; Capacity Building Frameworks and Pro-integrity Initiatives and Regional Economic Integration. Key outcomes include the adoption of the &#x201c;Tunis Declaration on Public Procurement Reforms in Africa&#x201d; which will lead to an established network of African procurement practitioners who are instrumental to the emergence of stronger public procurement institutions.  The expected impact is a reformed efficient and transparent public procurement system which promotes principles of good governance and accountability in public finance for improved social services and poverty reduction in RMCs. The forum was followed by a meeting in September 2010 of the Technical Committee of Procurement Experts (TCPE) who deliberated on the legal and operational documents for the Public Procurement Network. Approved: CAD 89,281 (75% disbursed and the balance is being returned to the TCA).</narrative>
  </description>
